  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">AA + readiness for El Nino in Somalia and Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">AA + readiness for El Nino in Somalia and Kenya</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Households and communities in flood prone areas in Kenya and Somalia, likely to be impacted by the negative impacts of predicted El Ni&#xf1;o-induced flooding,</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia and Kenya</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Somalia RCS  and Kenya RCS has a contingency Plan for Flood, drought and Cholera. In addition, IFRC will work with the National Societies to strengthen coordination with other actors and participate in relevant platforms. IFRC will conduct monitoring visits and organize meetings for NS to share experience during the implementation process eg through the ADMAG.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The likelihood of El Ni&#xf1;o in 2026 has now been confirmed with a confidence level over 90%. The potential impact will include: 
&#xf0a7;	Heavy &amp; above-normal rainfall leads to flooding, especially in the riverine areas. 
&#xf0a7;	Disruption of agricultural production leads to severe food insecurity and malnutrition 
&#xf0a7;	Spread of water-borne diseases, especially cholera and Acute Watery Diarrhoea (AWD)
&#xf0a7;	Displacement of communities from riverine areas leads to an increase in IDPs living in informal settlements and camps with no basic services. 
&#xf0a7;	Protection risks affecting women, children, and vulnerable populations</narrative>
  </description>
